const title       = 'Do Ravens Make Good Pets? The Honest Answer';
const description = 'Ravens are wild animals with complex social, cognitive, and foraging needs that cannot be met in captivity. They are also legally protected in most countries. This page gives an honest assessment of raven keeping.';
const path        = '/questions/do-ravens-make-good-pets/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Make Good Pets?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can you legally keep a raven as a pet?', a: 'In most countries, keeping wild Common Ravens is illegal without specific permits. In the United States, ravens are protected under the Migratory Bird Treaty Act — it is illegal to possess a wild raven, its eggs, feathers, or nest without a federal permit. In the UK, ravens are protected under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. Similar legislation applies across Europe. Some jurisdictions permit captive ravens in licensed wildlife rehabilitation or falconry contexts, but keeping a raven as a household pet is generally not legally possible without extensive permits rarely granted to private individuals.' },
  { q: 'What are ravens like as captive birds?', a: 'Historically, ravens have been kept captive by falconers and naturalists, and accounts (including Konrad Lorenz\'s detailed writing) describe ravens forming strong individual bonds with their keepers. They are highly intelligent, curious, and capable of learning. However, captive ravens require extensive stimulation, large flight spaces, social interaction (ideally with other ravens), varied diet, and mental challenges — needs that are very difficult to meet in a typical home environment. Under-stimulated captive ravens become destructive, aggressive, or develop stereotyped behaviours.' },
  { q: 'Did Konrad Lorenz keep ravens?', a: 'Yes. Konrad Lorenz, the Nobel laureate ethologist, kept and studied ravens (along with many other species) for decades and wrote about them in his popular book "King Solomon\'s Ring" (1949). His accounts of raven behaviour — their intelligence, play, and social bonds with people — are some of the most detailed early observations of corvid cognition. Lorenz\'s ravens were kept in naturalistic, free-flight conditions on his rural property, not as house pets. His observations contributed significantly to what we now understand about raven intelligence.' },
];
