    <h1>Do Ravens Make Good Pets? The Honest Answer</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> No — ravens do not make good pets for most people, and keeping them is illegal without specific permits in most countries. Ravens are highly intelligent wild birds with complex social, cognitive, foraging, and spatial needs that cannot be met in a typical domestic environment. Under-stimulated captive ravens become destructive and stressed. The legal, ethical, and practical barriers to keeping ravens are substantial.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Legal Status: Most Countries Prohibit It</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Wild Common Ravens are legally protected in most of their range:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>USA:</strong> protected under the Migratory Bird Treaty Act (MBTA). Illegal to possess a wild raven or its feathers, eggs, or nest without a federal permit. No "pet raven" permits are routinely issued to private individuals.</li>
      <li><strong>UK:</strong> protected under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. Illegal to intentionally kill, injure, or take a wild raven.</li>
      <li><strong>EU:</strong> protected under the Birds Directive (Directive 2009/147/EC). Member states may not permit capture except under specific scientific or conservation circumstances.</li>
      <li><strong>Canada:</strong> protected under the Migratory Birds Convention Act.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Captive-bred ravens (from licensed breeding facilities) may be legal to own in some jurisdictions, but sourcing one is difficult and legitimate breeders are extremely rare.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Need in Captivity</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Meeting a raven's welfare needs in captivity requires:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Large flight space:</strong> ravens in the wild fly many kilometres daily. Even large garden aviaries cannot provide adequate flight exercise.</li>
      <li><strong>Social stimulation:</strong> ravens are social birds. A lone raven with minimal human interaction will suffer. Even a raven bonded to a human keeper needs conspecific contact.</li>
      <li><strong>Cognitive enrichment:</strong> ravens have high cognitive needs. Without mental challenge — puzzle feeders, novel objects, foraging tasks, social interaction — they become bored and destructive.</li>
      <li><strong>Appropriate diet:</strong> ravens eat a varied omnivore diet including carrion, invertebrates, eggs, and meat. Maintaining this diet is costly and demanding.</li>
      <li><strong>Veterinary care:</strong> specialist avian veterinary care is required, and few veterinary practices have specific raven expertise.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Under-stimulated captive ravens are known to destroy property, become aggressive towards humans and other animals, develop repetitive stereotyped behaviours, and become severely stressed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Historical Accounts of Captive Ravens</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Ravens have been kept captive for millennia — by Viking-age Norse people, by falconers, by naturalists, and in historic zoos and estates. Konrad Lorenz's accounts of his free-living captive ravens in <em>King Solomon's Ring</em> (1949) describe birds that formed strong bonds with people, learned complex behaviours, and showed striking intelligence — but Lorenz's conditions (large property, free-flight, naturalistic conditions) are not replicable by most private keepers.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

